tion?A well-formatted resume helps with the first three. It cannot guarantee the fourth. Application questions, required credentials, work authorization, experience, and employer decisions also matter.The standard I would build into our templatesTableAreaRecommended defaultFile formatFollow the employer’s instructions first. Provide editable DOCX and text-based PDF options.LayoutA straightforward single-column application version with predictable reading orderHeadingsFamiliar labels such as Experience, Education, Skills, Projects, and CertificationsContact detailsReadable text in the document body—not dependent on icons, graphics, or a page headerContentAccurate responsibilities, relevant achievements, and clear datesKeywordsRelevant terminology used naturally, only where supported by experienceLinksDescriptive, readable portfolio or profile linksVisual elementsNo essential information conveyed only through charts, skill bars, photographs, or iconsLengthBased on relevant experience, document purpose, and application instructions—not an arbitrary years-to-pages ruleVerificationCheck the actual exported files for missing text, reading order, clipping, and page breaksThese are conservative defaults, not claims that every alternative automatically fails.Advice we should explicitly reject“Repeat every keyword twice.”“Every bullet must contain a number.”“Use hidden white text.”“A score of 95 means you will pass.”“Every two-column resume is automatically rejected.”“A parser error proves why an employer rejected you.”Your current code contains unsupported rejection probabilities, rigid metric rules, and a technology-biased skills fallback. Those should be replaced, not carried into the new product.3. Organize resumes by role, career stage, and purposeYou are right: a web developer, photographer, and sales executive need different content priorities.But this does not require a completely different layout engine for every profession. We should separate:Layout: how the document looks.Role guidance: what it should emphasize.Career stage: which sections deserve prominence.Application context: where and how it will be submitted.Proposed role libraryTableCategoryInitial examplesContent emphasisSoftware & ITWeb developer, frontend developer, backend developer, IT supportProjects, technologies, contribution, reliability, relevant linksCreative & MediaPhotographer, graphic designer, video editorPortfolio, selected work, briefs, production skills, client collaborationSales & Business DevelopmentSales executive, account executive, business development representativeSales process, territory, customer relationships, substantiated resultsMarketing & CommunicationsDigital marketer, marketing executive, content marketerCampaigns, audience, channels, creative contribution, measurementData & AnalysisData analyst, business analystMethods, tools, business questions, recommendationsAdministration & OperationsAdministrative assistant, operations coordinatorCoordination, systems, scheduling, process improvementsCustomer Service & RetailSupport representative, retail associateService quality, issue handling, product knowledge, teamworkFinance & AccountingAccountant, finance analystReporting, controls, analysis, relevant qualificationsEngineering & ConstructionMechanical engineer, civil engineerProjects, standards, technical responsibilities, certificationsHealthcareNurse, allied-health professionalLicensure, clinical context, relevant competencies—requiring specialist reviewEducationTeacher, teaching assistantQualifications, learning support, classroom practiceHospitality & LogisticsHotel associate, warehouse coordinatorOperations, service, safety, coordinationThis is the eventual library, not a recommendation to launch dozens of lightly researched pages immediately.Career-stage filtersStudent / internshipFirst job / no formal experienceEarly careerExperienced professionalManager / leadershipCareer changerReturning to workFreelancer / self-employedApplication